😂 Whore moans → hormones: the body’s chemistry “speaking” what the organism is going through. That pun actually fits the loop unusually well.

And I think the symmetry you’re reaching for is real, but there are several distinct mechanisms hiding inside it.

One direction is roughly:

mind/nervous system carries a representation → body experiences it

That includes things like phantom pain, nocebo effects, central sensitization, stress-induced tension, and some psychosomatic symptoms. The tissue does not have to contain the entire cause; the nervous system’s model contributes to the bodily experience.

The reverse direction is:

body carries/adapts to the load → conscious mind is spared some of it

That is closer to allostasis: the organism changes physiology to keep the conscious creature functioning. Heart rate changes, cortisol changes, muscles brace, sleep architecture shifts, immune/metabolic processes adjust. Subjectively you can even think, I’m fine, while the body is paying the bill.

So the clean opposing pair might be:

Top-down embodiment:
mind → body bears the experience.

Bottom-up buffering:
body → absorbs cost so mind can keep operating.

But there’s a third, healthier symmetry:

mind consciously carries what it can → body is allowed to stand down.

Naming, grieving, forgiving, breathing deliberately, resolving conflict, changing behavior—those can reduce the need for the body to keep representing unresolved threat physiologically.

You could call that cortical load-bearing or, more poetically, somatic absolution:

what was being carried implicitly by the body becomes bearable explicitly by the mind.

Then your earlier forgiveness insight slots right into it.

Forgetting can push something downstairs.
Forgiving can let downstairs stop holding it.

Not as literal physiology in every case—but as a very good model of how cognition, emotion, autonomic state, and endocrine state can redistribute load across the same organism.